# Runtime artifacts. Never tracked.
#
# Worker logs contain full task text and model output, and .state files are
# per-host resume pointers -- neither is source, and both would leak operator
# content into history. Environment files are excluded so a credential cannot
# reach a commit even by accident (Phase 0 requirement: credentials are loaded
# from the environment and must not be committable).

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/

*.log
*.state

# Written before a model runs and cleared after its result is reported, so a
# leftover one means the process died mid-task. Per-host runtime state, never
# source -- and committing one would tell every other checkout that a task it
# has never heard of was interrupted.
*.inflight

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.secret
secrets.json

# The control directory. Per-host runtime state, and none of it is source.
#
# Six of these were committed by accident on the supervisor branch: three
# `.pid` locks, `narration.jsonl`, `status.json`, and `supervisor.out`. The
# logs and chat content were the obvious cost. The dangerous one was the pid
# files. A lock file names a pid that this host is willing to terminate during
# shutdown, so a committed one hands every other checkout a number pointing at
# a process that machine never started -- and pids are reused. Cleanup would
# then kill whatever inherited it.
#
# Shutdown now confirms a pid is really that identity's worker before
# terminating it, so the lock file alone is no longer authority to kill. This
# keeps the number from travelling in the first place, which is the half that
# does not depend on a guard being correct.
#
# The whole directory rather than a list: activations, consumed activations,
# the pause and stop flags, status, narration and locks are all written by a
# running swarm and read by nothing outside this host. Nothing under it has
# ever been source, so a pattern that has to be extended every time the
# runtime grows a file is the wrong shape.
control/

# Runtime usage tracking file. Per-host state, not source.
# This file tracks model usage for rate limiting.
claude_worker.usage

# Rate limit hold-off marker. Per-host state, not source.
claude_worker.ratelimit
